>
  <p>Spring does not need to begin with an entire weekend of cleaning.</p>
  <p>Sometimes one small job is enough to make a room feel noticeably better. Pick one task, spend around 20 minutes on it, and stop when it is done.</p>
  <p>If you feel like doing another one later, great. If not, you still improved something.</p>

  <h2>1. Clear one kitchen drawer</h2>
  <p>Do not reorganise the whole kitchen. Pick one drawer that has become difficult to use.</p>
  <p>Take everything out, remove rubbish or items that belong elsewhere, wipe the drawer if needed, and return only what you use in that space. If it reveals a bigger storage problem, leave that for later and use the <a href="/guides/kitchen-drawer-storage-what-to-measure-before-buying-organisers/">kitchen drawer storage guide</a> to plan it properly.</p>

  <h2>2. Check one pantry shelf</h2>
  <p>Choose a single shelf rather than emptying the entire pantry. Look for expired food, duplicate open packets, ingredients pushed to the back and containers that no longer seal properly.</p>
  <p>Bring food that needs using soon towards the front. This quick reset makes one shelf easier to use; the <a href="/guides/pantry-storage-ideas-australian-kitchens/">pantry storage guide</a> can help if poor visibility or deep shelves keep causing the same problem.</p>

  <h2>3. Reset the area under the kitchen sink</h2>
  <p>Remove anything that does not belong, check for obvious moisture or leaks, and group the products you actually use. Keep products in their original containers and do not mix cleaning chemicals or store incompatible products together.</p>
  <p>If pipes, hinges or moisture make the space awkward, check the <a href="/guides/under-sink-kitchen-storage-what-to-check-before-you-buy/">under-sink kitchen storage guide</a> before adding organisers.</p>

  <h2>4. Clean the washing-machine area</h2>
  <p>Clear detergent spills, remove lint and dust from accessible surfaces, and tidy products stored around the machine. Return empty bottles, odd socks and miscellaneous supplies to the right place rather than letting the laundry become a dumping ground.</p>
  <p>For a larger reset, use the <a href="/guides/laundry-storage-what-to-measure-and-check-before-you-buy/">laundry storage guide</a> to protect appliance access and everyday working space.</p>

  <h2>5. Clear the bathroom vanity</h2>
  <p>Choose the drawer, shelf or benchtop that annoys you most. Dispose of empty packaging, group frequently used items and move rarely used products out of prime storage.</p>
  <p>Check for leaks or damaged cupboard surfaces as you go. The <a href="/guides/bathroom-vanity-storage-what-to-sort-before-buying-organisers/">bathroom vanity storage guide</a> covers the bigger sorting and measuring job if one small zone is not enough.</p>

  <h2>6. Wash or check one flyscreen</h2>
  <p>Flyscreens matter more once windows stay open again. Choose one frequently used window or door, remove loose dust and debris using a method suitable for the screen, and look for damaged mesh or loose edges.</p>
  <p>If it needs repair, add that to your maintenance list. Do not let today’s small clean turn into an unplanned repair project.</p>

  <h2>7. Clean one ceiling fan</h2>
  <p>Turn the fan off and make sure it cannot start unexpectedly. Clean accessible blades using a safe method for the fan and your access; never climb on unstable furniture.</p>
  <p>If the fan moves or sounds unusual when running, stop and use the <a href="/guides/ceiling-fans-before-you-buy/">ceiling fan guide</a> to understand the checks and professional-work boundaries.</p>

  <h2>8. Check one air-conditioner filter</h2>
  <p>If the manufacturer’s instructions identify the filter as owner-accessible, check whether it needs cleaning and follow the directions for your model. Do not assume every filter is washable.</p>
  <p>Do not dismantle the indoor unit or attempt electrical or refrigeration work. The <a href="/guides/air-conditioner-maintenance-before-summer/">air-conditioner maintenance guide</a> explains the owner checks to make before the first genuinely hot day.</p>

  <h2>9. Clear one problem surface</h2>
  <p>Choose one bench, console, bedside table or other flat surface that has become a collection point. Put away anything with an obvious home and throw away rubbish.</p>
  <p>If an item has no home, decide whether it needs one rather than moving the pile elsewhere. The objective is one usable surface. For a kitchen collection point, the <a href="/guides/kitchen-bench-space-what-to-clear-before-buying-more-storage/">clear bench space guide</a> provides a useful next step.</p>

  <h2>10. Sort one small pile of clothes</h2>
  <p>Do not attempt the whole wardrobe. Pick one manageable category: winter jumpers, work clothes, shoes, donations or the clothes sitting on a chair.</p>
  <p>Put away what still belongs, set aside genuine donations and remove anything damaged beyond useful repair. A full seasonal changeover can wait; use the <a href="/guides/bedroom-wardrobe-organisation-what-to-sort-before-buying-storage/">wardrobe storage guide</a> when you are ready to sort the larger space.</p>

  <h2>11. Reset one outdoor sitting area</h2>
  <p>If you have a balcony, patio or outdoor table, remove leaves and debris, wipe the table, check furniture for obvious damage and put away items that accumulated over winter.</p>
  <p>Keep the job to one usable area. The <a href="/guides/outdoor-entertaining-area-setup-what-to-plan-before-buying-extra-furniture-and-accessories/">outdoor entertaining guide</a> can help later if the layout, shade or storage needs more thought.</p>

  <h2>12. Make a five-item repair list</h2>
  <p>Note five small things you have been ignoring, such as a loose cabinet hinge, torn flyscreen, dripping tap, failed light bulb or damaged door seal. Keep the list short and record the problem without turning this session into five repairs.</p>
  <p>Electrical faults, structural problems, refrigerant systems and anything requiring unsafe access belong with an appropriately qualified professional.</p>

  <h2>You do not have to do all 12</h2>
  <p>This is not another checklist to finish. Choose the task that would make the biggest difference to your home today and do that one.</p>
  <p>For a comprehensive room-by-room reset, use the <a href="/guides/spring-cleaning-checklist/">Spring Cleaning Checklist</a>. For inspections and pre-summer preparation, use the <a href="/guides/spring-home-maintenance-checklist/">Spring Home Maintenance Checklist for Australian Homes</a>.</p>
  <p>One drawer, one shelf or one neglected corner is still progress.</p>

